.TH CHMAP_CONSTRUCT_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_construct_scoped \- declare and create a hash map that is destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_construct_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a hash map handle named hm_name. It also declares the companion
type variables. These variables carry key_t and val_t to every later
type-inferred macro. The macro then creates the map with the default allocator
and with the map's own hashing for key_t. It also attaches the cleanup
attribute. This attribute destroys the map at every exit from the enclosing
scope, an early return included.

This macro is chmap_construct(3) plus that cleanup attribute. Nothing else
is different: the same allocator, the same hashing, and the same choice of
implementation strategy from key_t and val_t. Use this macro when the whole
useful life of the map is one block. Use chmap_construct(3) when the caller
returns the handle, stores it, or destroys it somewhere that the compiler
cannot see.

chmap_declare_scoped(3) and then chmap_init(3) is the two-step form. Use
the two-step form for a map whose configuration is decided inside the
scope, and not at the declaration.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name for the map handle to declare
key_t    Key type; it tells every later macro how to read a key and, with
         val_t, which implementation strategy the map uses
val_t    Value type
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails. Use
chmap_declare_scoped(3) with chmap_init(3) when the caller must handle a
failure
Needs GCC or Clang, because it uses the cleanup attribute
Uses the default allocator and CCOL_DEFAULT_INITIAL_BUCKET_ARRAY_SIZE (16)
buckets
Scope exit destroys the map and clears the handle. An explicit
chmap_destroy(3) earlier in the scope therefore stays correct, and the
cleanup at scope exit then does nothing
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle. A function
that gets the handle therefore needs chmap_redeclare(3) there
Destruction of the map frees the copies of the keys and values that it
owns. It does not free anything that a stored pointer value points at
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The handle must not outlive its scope. Do not return the handle. Do not
store it somewhere that outlives the scope. Both of these hand out a handle
whose map is freed at the exit from the scope.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
int count_unique(char **words, size_t n) {
    chmap_construct_scoped(seen, char *, int);
    int one = 1;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        chmap_insert(seen, words[i], one);
    }
    return (int)chmap_elem_count(seen);   /* seen is destroyed here */
}
.fi
